<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Rada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Central Council of Ukraine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> was the Ukrainian revolutionary council formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in March 1917 under the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykhailo-hrushevsky">Mykhailo Hrushevsky</a>. Through its Universals it proclaimed Ukrainian autonomy, then the Ukrainian People's Republic, and full independence in January 1918. It signed a separate peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, who dissolved it in April 1918 in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavlo-skoropadsky">Pavlo Skoropadsky</a>'s Hetmanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-rada.jpg" alt="Central Rada" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Tora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_UNR.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>News of the February events in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in March 1917, and Ukrainian organisations met at the Rodyna Club. More than 100 representatives gathered on 16 March (3 March in the old style), and the Central Council was founded the next day on the initiative of the Society of Ukrainian Progressionists, with other parties and organisations taking part. It told the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-provisional-government">Russian Provisional Government</a> of its creation by telegram. On 20 March Mykhailo Hrushevsky was elected its head, with Dmytro Doroshenko and Dmytro Antonovych as his deputies. After the All-Ukrainian National Congress of 19 to 21 April 1917 it became a parliament of 150 members elected from parties, professional and cultural organisations and the guberniyas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its four Universals led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from autonomy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The First, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-vynnychenko">Volodymyr Vynnychenko</a> and proclaimed on 23 June 1917, declared that the Ukrainian people must manage their own lives without separating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the General Secretariat was formed on 28 June. The Second, of 16 July, enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> with representatives of other peoples living in Ukraine. After the Bolshevik coup in Russia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-universal">Third Universal</a> of 20 November 1917 proclaimed the Ukrainian People's Republic in federal ties with the Russian Republic. In elections to the Russian Constituent Assembly on 25 November, Ukrainian parties won 75% of the vote and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> 11%.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> made several attempts to seize power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The Russian government sent an ultimatum on 17 December, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> rejected, and Bolshevik troops began an offensive. Bolshevik deputies withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> and formed a rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat">People's Secretariat</a> on 25 December. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-universal">Fourth Universal</a> declared the Ukrainian People's Republic a separate, free and sovereign state, and the Rada's delegation went to the peace conference with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a>. On 29 January 1918 Bolshevik troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>.</p><p>By the end of July 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> formally had 822 deputies, according to Pavlo Khrystiuk, among them delegates of peasants, soldiers and workers and of Ukrainian, Russian, Jewish and Polish parties. Its executive, the Little Rada or Mala Rada, was created in April 1917 with 19 members and chaired by Hrushevsky. The general assembly met nine times and was modelled on the Russian State Duma, with party factions and inquiries. It has been judged inefficient, its sessions often given over to verbal battles between factions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Soviet ideology described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> as a counter-revolutionary body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>. Its members were proclaimed outlaws by the Soviet government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in December 1917. Many went into exile, among them Vynnychenko, who died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, while others were imprisoned or shot in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and Hrushevsky died in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Central Rada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In March 1917 Ukrainian parties formed the <b>Central Rada</b> in Kyiv, chaired by the historian Mykhailo Hrushevsky.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1917, after Bolshevik delegates walked out of a congress of soviets in Kiev dominated by supporters of the <b>Central Rada</b>, they held their own congress in Kharkiv.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic-of-soviets">Ukrainian People's Republic of Soviets</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 April 1918 German occupation authorities, unhappy with the socialist <b>Central Rada</b> of the Ukrainian People's Republic, supported a coup by General Pavlo Skoropadsky.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-state">Ukrainian State</a></li></ul></section>
